#  Damage-limited compositing: enable WebKit's compiled-in-but-WPE-disabled
#  damage subsystem so a composite is scissored to the region that actually
#  changed instead of redrawing the whole scene (WEBKIT_DAMAGE_COMPOSITING,
#  default OFF in runtime-common.sh). Only touches LayerTreeHost.cpp; must
#  apply AFTER every other patch that touches that file (raster-on-compositor,
#  the composite-scroll-sync trio, fling-throttle) so its context matches the
#  fully-patched tree.
#
# ---
Damage-limited compositing: scissor the scene composite to the changed region.

WPE compiles WebKit's damage-tracking subsystem in (ENABLE_DAMAGE_TRACKING=1)
but leaves it disabled at runtime: the three WebCore settings that drive it
default to false for every non-GTK port, and WPE exposes no WebPreferences
plumbing to flip them. So today every composited frame - including every scroll
frame - redraws the whole scene clipped only to the full viewport, even when a
tiny region (a caret, a spinner, a playing video, a settled post-scroll repaint)
is all that changed.

The machinery to do better is already present and wired: GraphicsLayerCoordinated
/ CoordinatedPlatformLayer produce per-layer dirty rects, ThreadedCompositor::
paintToCurrentGLContext() aggregates them into one frame Damage and - when the
DamagePropagationFlags carry UseForCompositing - fetches m_surface->
renderTargetDamage() (the damage accumulated for the *specific* buffer about to
be reused, i.e. buffer-age aware, so partial composite into a rotated buffer is
correct) and beginClip()s the TextureMapper paint to that region via glScissor.
CoordinatedBackingStore additionally draws only the tile x damage intersection
for opaque tiles at identity transform. All of this is gated behind flags that
LayerTreeHost never turns on for WPE.

This patch makes LayerTreeHost honour env overrides so the stack can be enabled
and A/B'd on-device without a preferences-plumbing change:

  WEBKIT_DAMAGE_COMPOSITING=1          master switch: propagate damage AND use it
                                       for compositing (the scissored composite).
  WEBKIT_DAMAGE_UNIFY=0|1              frame-damage mode when forced on: 1 (default)
                                       = single bounding box; 0 = per-rect (Rectangles).
  WEBKIT_DAMAGE_USE_FOR_COMPOSITING=1  when forced on, whether to scissor the
                                       composite (default 1). Set 0 to propagate
                                       damage to the UI process only, without the
                                       WebProcess-side composite clip - lets us
                                       separate the two effects during A/B.

Default OFF (behaviour unchanged unless WEBKIT_DAMAGE_COMPOSITING is set). An
off-by-default flag is NOT assumed safe here; this must be device-verified
(correctness first - watch for stale pixels outside the scissor - then perf)
before it is considered for shipping on.

Note on scroll specifically: an async scroll moves the scrolling layer via a
transform change, which self-damages the whole layer, so the frame-damage bounds
collapse to ~the full viewport and the bbox scissor buys little *during* an
active fling. The immediate win is every non-scroll and settled-frame repaint
that currently recomposites the entire scene. Tightening the scroll case itself
(per-rect scissor / layer-cull in the paint traversal / retained-target scroll-
copy) is a follow-up that builds on this being on.

--- a/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/CoordinatedGraphics/LayerTreeHost.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/CoordinatedGraphics/LayerTreeHost.cpp
@@ -103,6 +103,31 @@
     return ms;
 }
 
+#if ENABLE(DAMAGE_TRACKING)
+namespace {
+// Atlantic: WPE compiles the damage subsystem in but leaves it disabled - the
+// WebCore settings default to false off-GTK and there is no WPE preference to
+// flip them. These env vars force it on so a composite can be scissored to the
+// region that actually changed instead of redrawing the whole scene each frame:
+//   WEBKIT_DAMAGE_COMPOSITING=1          master switch (propagate damage + use it for compositing)
+//   WEBKIT_DAMAGE_UNIFY=0|1              bounding-box (1, default) vs per-rect (0) frame damage
+//   WEBKIT_DAMAGE_USE_FOR_COMPOSITING=1  scissor the scene composite to the changed region (default 1)
+// Off unless WEBKIT_DAMAGE_COMPOSITING is set. An off flag is not assumed safe:
+// this must be device-verified before it is ever shipped on.
+bool damageEnvFlag(const char* name, bool defaultValue)
+{
+    const char* v = getenv(name);
+    return v ? (*v && *v != '0') : defaultValue;
+}
+
+bool damageForceOn()
+{
+    static const bool on = damageEnvFlag("WEBKIT_DAMAGE_COMPOSITING", false);
+    return on;
+}
+} // namespace
+#endif
+
